This question takes two decisions for granted, so I've made defaults for both. You can change them.

- **HaveIBeenPwned API vs. a local copy of the list.** The API adds a network dependency and latency to every check. A local list avoids that and keeps hashes in-house, but you have to download, store, and refresh it. I defaulted to the API's k-anonymity endpoint behind a small interface, so you can swap in a local lookup later.
- **What happens at login when the password is breached.** Blocking outright, forcing a reset, and requiring step-up are different tradeoffs. I defaulted to forcing a reset before any session is issued. Since your attackers are logging in with the right passwords, this stops them without you needing to know who is who. The policy is a single switch.

```ts
import { createHash } from "node:crypto";

// ---- Breach lookup (swap implementation without touching callers) ----
export interface BreachChecker {
  /** Returns breach count (0 = not found). Throws on lookup failure. */
  count(password: string): Promise<number>;
}

export class HibpRangeChecker implements BreachChecker {
  private cache = new Map<string, { suffixes: Map<string, number>; at: number }>();
  constructor(
    private timeoutMs = 1500,
    private cacheTtlMs = 60 * 60 * 1000
  ) {}

  async count(password: string): Promise<number> {
    const sha1 = createHash("sha1").update(password, "utf8").digest("hex").toUpperCase();
    const prefix = sha1.slice(0, 5);
    const suffix = sha1.slice(5);

    let entry = this.cache.get(prefix);
    if (!entry || Date.now() - entry.at > this.cacheTtlMs) {
      const res = await fetch(`https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/${prefix}`, {
        headers: { "Add-Padding": "true", "User-Agent": "YourApp-breach-check" },
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(this.timeoutMs),
      });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HIBP range lookup failed: ${res.status}`);

      const suffixes = new Map<string, number>();
      for (const line of (await res.text()).split("\n")) {
        const [s, n] = line.trim().split(":");
        if (s && n) suffixes.set(s, parseInt(n, 10));
      }
      entry = { suffixes, at: Date.now() };
      this.cache.set(prefix, entry);
    }
    // Padded entries have count 0, so they never count as a match.
    return entry.suffixes.get(suffix) ?? 0;
  }
}

// ---- Policy wrapper ----
export type BreachResult =
  | { status: "ok" }
  | { status: "breached"; count: number }
  | { status: "unknown" }; // lookup failed

export async function checkPassword(
  checker: BreachChecker,
  password: string,
  opts: { minCount?: number } = {}
): Promise<BreachResult> {
  const minCount = opts.minCount ?? 1;
  try {
    const n = await checker.count(password);
    return n >= minCount ? { status: "breached", count: n } : { status: "ok" };
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn("breach check unavailable", (err as Error).message); // never log the password
    return { status: "unknown" };
  }
}
```

**Where to call it**

```ts
const checker = new HibpRangeChecker();

// 1) Signup / password change / reset: reject before saving.
//    Fail open on "unknown" so an HIBP outage doesn't block signups.
const r = await checkPassword(checker, newPassword);
if (r.status === "breached") {
  return res.status(422).json({
    error: "password_breached",
    message: "This password appears in known data breaches. Choose a different one.",
  });
}

// 2) Login: run only AFTER the password has been verified as correct.
//    This is the only moment you have the plaintext.
const LOGIN_POLICY: "force_reset" | "step_up" | "warn" = "force_reset";

const r2 = await checkPassword(checker, submittedPassword);
if (r2.status === "breached") {
  await db.users.update(user.id, { must_reset_password: true });
  await revokeAllSessions(user.id);
  await sendPasswordResetEmail(user.id); // link goes to the address on file, not the login response

  if (LOGIN_POLICY === "force_reset") {
