One catch first: the sleep example I gave uses deep sleep, which resets the CPU and wipes normal RAM. A buffer in an ordinary `static` array would be empty on every wake. So this buffer lives in RTC slow memory (`RTC_DATA_ATTR`), which survives deep sleep.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "esp_attr.h"

#define BUF_CAPACITY  128                 /* 128 * 8 B = 1 KB; RTC slow mem is 8 KB total */
#define BUF_MAGIC     0xB0FFE12AU

typedef struct {
    uint32_t timestamp_s;                 /* seconds (e.g. from time()) */
    int32_t  value;                       /* scaled integer, e.g. centi-degrees C */
} reading_t;

typedef struct {
    uint32_t  magic;
    uint16_t  head;                       /* index of oldest entry */
    uint16_t  count;                      /* entries currently stored */
    uint32_t  dropped;                    /* overwritten because buffer was full */
    reading_t items[BUF_CAPACITY];
} reading_buf_t;

static RTC_DATA_ATTR reading_buf_t s_buf;

/* Call once at the top of app_main(). After a deep-sleep wake the data is
 * intact and this does nothing; after power-on/brownout it resets the buffer. */
void readings_init(void)
{
    if (s_buf.magic != BUF_MAGIC ||
        s_buf.head  >= BUF_CAPACITY ||
        s_buf.count >  BUF_CAPACITY) {
        memset(&s_buf, 0, sizeof s_buf);
        s_buf.magic = BUF_MAGIC;
    }
}

/* Append a reading. If full, the oldest reading is overwritten. */
void readings_add(uint32_t timestamp_s, int32_t value)
{
    if (s_buf.count == BUF_CAPACITY) {
        s_buf.head = (s_buf.head + 1) % BUF_CAPACITY;
        s_buf.count--;
        s_buf.dropped++;
    }
    uint16_t tail = (s_buf.head + s_buf.count) % BUF_CAPACITY;
    s_buf.items[tail].timestamp_s = timestamp_s;
    s_buf.items[tail].value       = value;
    s_buf.count++;
}

uint16_t readings_count(void)   { return s_buf.count; }
uint32_t readings_dropped(void) { return s_buf.dropped; }

/* Copy the i-th oldest reading (0 = oldest). Returns false if out of range. */
bool readings_get(uint16_t i, reading_t *out)
{
    if (i >= s_buf.count) return false;
    *out = s_buf.items[(s_buf.head + i) % BUF_CAPACITY];
    return true;
}

/* Remove the n oldest readings. Call only after they were sent successfully. */
void readings_discard(uint16_t n)
{
    if (n > s_buf.count) n = s_buf.count;
    s_buf.head = (s_buf.head + n) % BUF_CAPACITY;
    s_buf.count -= n;
}
```

Usage:

```c
#define SAMPLE_PERIOD_S   60
#define SAMPLES_PER_TX    10        /* transmit every 10 minutes */

void app_main(void)
{
    readings_init();

    readings_add((uint32_t)time(NULL), read_sensor_scaled());

    if (readings_count() >= SAMPLES_PER_TX) {
        uint16_t sent = 0;
        reading_t r;
        if (radio_connect() == ESP_OK) {
            while (readings_get(sent, &r) && radio_send(&r) == ESP_OK) {
                sent++;
            }
            radio_disconnect();
        }
        readings_discard(sent);     /* keep anything that failed for next time */
    }

    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(set_wake_timer(SAMPLE_PERIOD_S));
    esp_deep_sleep_start();
}
```

Notes:

- **Sample often, transmit rarely.** This is where the power savings come from. A sample-only wake takes tens of milliseconds with the radio off, while a Wi-Fi connect and send takes seconds at 80 mA or more. Tune `SAMPLES_PER_TX` against your data-freshness needs.
- **Failed sends are retried.** Only the readings actually sent are discarded. If the link is down for a long time, the buffer overwrites the oldest data, and `readings_dropped()` tells you how many were lost.
- **RTC memory doesn't survive power loss, a brownout, or a hard reset.** If losing a buffer matters, you'd need to write to flash (NVS or a log partition). That costs energy and wears the flash, so batch the writes.
- **Timestamps:** `time()` keeps running through deep sleep, but the RTC slow clock drifts (roughly a percent for the internal RC oscillator). If you need accuracy, sync via SNTP at some of the connections, or store a wake counter and reconstruct the times on the server.
- **This isn't thread-safe.** It assumes one task, which fits a wake, sample, sleep flow. If you add tasks or ISRs that call `readings_add`, you'll need a mutex or critical section.

If you're on light sleep rather than deep sleep, RAM is preserved, and you can drop `RTC_DATA_ATTR` and use a larger buffer.

