Before the sketch, I need to correct my previous usage example. If `radio_connect()` fails, `readings_count()` stays at or above `SAMPLES_PER_TX`, so the device retries the connection on every 60-second wake. Each attempt can burn several seconds at ~80 mA or more, so a router outage would drain the battery quickly, which is the problem you're trying to fix. The sketch below adds a backoff.

With deep sleep there is no real loop. Each wake restarts `app_main()`, so the cycle is a single pass of wake, sample, maybe transmit, and sleep.

```c
#include <time.h>
#include "esp_sleep.h"
#include "esp_attr.h"

#define SAMPLE_PERIOD_S    60
#define SAMPLES_PER_TX     10
#define MAX_BACKOFF_WAKES  60          /* never wait more than ~1 h between retries */

/* Survive deep sleep */
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint32_t s_wake_count;
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint32_t s_next_tx_wake;   /* don't transmit before this wake */
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint8_t  s_fail_streak;

static bool tx_due(void)
{
    return readings_count() >= SAMPLES_PER_TX &&
           s_wake_count     >= s_next_tx_wake;
}

static void note_tx_result(bool ok)
{
    if (ok) {
        s_fail_streak  = 0;
        s_next_tx_wake = 0;
    } else {
        if (s_fail_streak < 8) s_fail_streak++;
        uint32_t wait = 1U << s_fail_streak;            /* 2, 4, 8, ... wakes */
        if (wait > MAX_BACKOFF_WAKES) wait = MAX_BACKOFF_WAKES;
        s_next_tx_wake = s_wake_count + wait;
    }
}

static bool try_transmit(void)
{
    if (radio_connect() != ESP_OK) return false;        /* give connect a hard timeout */

    uint16_t sent = 0;
    reading_t r;
    while (readings_get(sent, &r) && radio_send(&r) == ESP_OK) {
        sent++;
    }
    radio_disconnect();
    readings_discard(sent);
    return readings_count() == 0;                        /* true only if all went out */
}

void app_main(void)
{
    s_wake_count++;
    readings_init();

    /* 1. Sample (radio is off; power the sensor only for the read) */
    sensor_power(true);
    int32_t v = read_sensor_scaled();
    sensor_power(false);
    readings_add((uint32_t)time(NULL), v);

    /* 2. Transmit only when due and not backing off */
    if (tx_due()) {
        note_tx_result(try_transmit());
    }

    /* 3. Sleep */
    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(set_wake_timer(SAMPLE_PERIOD_S));
    esp_deep_sleep_start();                              /* never returns */
}
```

Details that affect the battery life:

- **Timeouts.** `radio_connect()` needs a hard cap, such as 5-10 s. Without one, a missing access point can keep the radio on for 30 s or more.
- **Backoff vs. buffer size.** At 60 s samples, the 128-entry buffer fills in about 2 hours. During a longer outage the oldest data is overwritten, and `readings_dropped()` counts the loss. If outages of that length are plausible, sample less often or use a bigger buffer.
- **Cycle timing.** The sleep timer starts after your work finishes, so the real period is 60 s plus awake time, and transmit wakes run longer. This is fine for trending, but it's one more reason to timestamp each reading.
- **Sleep current.** Measure it with a meter after you build this. Typical culprits are a sensor that stays powered, floating or pulled GPIOs, a USB-serial chip on the dev board, and a regulator with high quiescent current. A bare ESP32 can sleep at around 10 µA, but a dev board often sits at several mA, which would cancel the benefit.
- **Low battery.** Consider reading battery voltage on each wake and stretching the sample and transmit intervals when it's low. That's cheap insurance for a six-month deployment.
- **First boot.** On power-up, `s_wake_count` and the other RTC variables are zero, so the first pass behaves normally. If you want a time sync at startup, check `esp_sleep_get_wakeup_cause()` and do a one-time connect and SNTP when the cause isn't the timer.

